d4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-124729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xz+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2xz+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-124729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12b7d1b64dd7cdd68f7177ee05fb7129c16fc63fa5ad07a171a784baabdb59b7"} {"question" "q-124729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-124729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x,y,z)=xy+{x}^{2}z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+yz+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-124729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-81752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=(yz{e}^{xz}+z)\\,i+({e}^{xz}+1)\\,j+(xy{e}^{xz}+x)\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-81752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=yz{e}^{xz}+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{xz}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{xz}+x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (yz{e}^{xz}+z)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =y{e}^{xz}+xz+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{xz}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{xz}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xz}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & ={e}^{xz}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{xz}+x+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{xz}+x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{xz}+x+{h}^{′}(z) & =zy{e}^{xz}+x \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+C.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-81752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12b7d1b64dd7cdd68f7177ee05fb7129c16fc63fa5ad07a171a784baabdb59b7"} {"question" "q-81752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-81752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x,y,z)=xz{e}^{xz}+xz+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=xz{e}^{xz}+y+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xy+z+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+yz+x+C"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-81752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+C"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-132100" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1)\\,i+(\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2})\\,j-2yz\\,k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-132100" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =-{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=-y{z}^{2}+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2yz & =-2yz+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+C.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-132100" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12b7d1b64dd7cdd68f7177ee05fb7129c16fc63fa5ad07a171a784baabdb59b7"} {"question" "q-132100" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-132100" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xy-y{z}^{2}+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xy+x{z}^{2}+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xz-{z}^{2}+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{x}^{2}-y{z}^{2}+C"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-132100" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+C"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-81751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=y{z}^{2}\\,i+(x{z}^{2}+z)\\,j+(2xyz+y-2)\\,k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-81751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{z}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{z}^{2}+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2xyz+y-2\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xy{z}^{2}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{z}^{2}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{z}^{2}+z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{z}^{2}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x{z}^{2}+z \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =yz+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=yz+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+yz+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2xyz+y+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2xyz+y-2$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xyz+y-2 & =2xyz+y+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =-2 \\\\ h(z) & =-2z+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+yz-2z+C.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-81751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12b7d1b64dd7cdd68f7177ee05fb7129c16fc63fa5ad07a171a784baabdb59b7"} {"question" "q-81751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-81751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x,y,z)=2xyz+y-2+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=2xyz+yz-2+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=2xy{z}^{2}+y-z+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=xyz+yz-z+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+yz-2z+C"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-81751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+yz-2z+C"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-132103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(2x+y{e}^{z})\\,i+(x{e}^{z}+1)\\,j+(xy{e}^{z}-2z)\\,k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-132103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}-2z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x+y{e}^{z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+xy{e}^{z}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x{e}^{z}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy{e}^{z}+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}-2z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{z}-2z & =xy{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =-2z \\\\ h(z) & =-{z}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+y-{z}^{2}+C.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-132103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12b7d1b64dd7cdd68f7177ee05fb7129c16fc63fa5ad07a171a784baabdb59b7"} {"question" "q-132103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-132103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x,y,z)=xyz{e}^{z}+x+y-{z}^{2}+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+x+y-z+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+x+{y}^{2}z+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+y-{z}^{2}+C"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{z}+y{x}^{2}+x-{z}^{2}+C"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "q-132103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+y-{z}^{2}+C"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/state.json" "file_sha256" "664c698c201658a8fa9bd4c5b315fd03bc3ce8c35e49e9e65cd91a37a6a91041"} {"question" "e-7020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf{F},$ where $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2xy\\ \\mathbf i + (x^2 - 3)\\ \\mathbf j .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/example-7020.json" "file_sha256" "98763055da067906bf08e3f88300e05867bae317d979fa5bc2903f7db1f1a27b"} {"question" "e-7020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\\,\\textbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}}\\,\\textbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2-3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 -3$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}-3 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =-3 \\\\ g(y) & =-3y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=-3y +C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2y -3y + C.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/example-7020.json" "file_sha256" "98763055da067906bf08e3f88300e05867bae317d979fa5bc2903f7db1f1a27b"} {"question" "e-7184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)  = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = yz\\,\\mathbf{i} + (xz + 4z^2)\\,\\mathbf{j} + (xy+8yz)\\,\\mathbf{k}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/example-7184.json" "file_sha256" "1fb3be7d064a4804c8766d1c67f758e444eaca0a47a0bae427f576c152dda7c4"} {"question" "e-7184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} \\, \\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)  = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = yz, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int yz\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xyz+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + \\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xz+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =xz+4{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z) = 4yz^2 + h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x, y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy + 8yz + h'(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy+8yz & =xy+8yz+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + C.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046299/example-7184.json" "file_sha256" "1fb3be7d064a4804c8766d1c67f758e444eaca0a47a0bae427f576c152dda7c4"}] "usage" {"e-7184" [] "q-35994" [] "e-7020" []} "basis" 1683 "retractions" [[17592186124859 :question/problem "Find $f(x, y)$ such that $∇f = F$, where $F(x, y) =-yi + (3 - x)j$."] [17592186124296 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf{F}$, where $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2xy\\ \\mathbf i + (x^2 - 3)\\ \\mathbf j$."] [17592186124296 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\\,\\textbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}}\\,\\textbf{j}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf F(x,y)\n$$\n\nthe function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2-3\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= ∫\\frac{∂f}{∂x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫2xydx \\\\\n&= x^{2}y + g(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 + g'(y)\n$$\n\nTaking\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 -3\n$$\n\ninto account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + g^{′}(y) &= x^{2} - 3 \\\\\ng^{′}(y) &= -3 \\\\\ng(y) &= -3y + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\ng(y)=-3y +C\n$$\n\ninto our expression for $f(x,y)$, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2y -3y + C\n$$"] [17592186124300 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)$, where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = yz\\,\\mathbf{i} + (xz + 4z^2)\\,\\mathbf{j} + (xy+8yz)\\,\\mathbf{k}$."] [17592186124300 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} \\, \\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nthe function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = yz, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y, z) &= ∫\\frac{∂f}{∂x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫yzdx \\\\\n&= xyz + g(y, z)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + \\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}\n$$\n\nTaking\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2\n$$\n\ninto account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nxz + \\frac{∂g}{∂y} &= xz + 4z^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{∂g}{∂y} &= 4z^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}\n$$\n\nwith respect to $y$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y, z) &= ∫\\frac{∂g}{∂y}dy \\\\\n&= ∫4z^{2}dy \\\\\n&= 4yz^{2} + h(z)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ng(y,z) = 4yz^2 + h(z)\n$$\n\nin the expression for $f(x, y,z)$, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + h(z)\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z)$, we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy + 8yz + h'(z)\n$$\n\nTaking\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz\n$$\n\ninto account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nxy + 8yz &= xy + 8yz + h^{′}(z) \\\\\nh^{′}(z) &= 0 \\\\\nh(z) &= C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + C\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122177 17592186122178 17592186122179 17592186122180 17592186122181] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122176 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122176 :answer-field/id] [17592186122176 :answer-field/key] [17592186122176 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
